Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 18:15:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] =?UTF-8?q?feat(db):=20phase=207=20=E2=80=94=20the=20o?=
 =?UTF-8?q?nboarding=20catalog,=20answer=20integrity,=20and=20one=20door?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20for=20the=20lifecycle?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three migrations, zero new tables, zero new columns. The phase-2 schema had
anticipated the whole phase; what was missing were boundaries.

The social catalog moves out of seed.sql and into a migration, with the SAME
stable ids. It is Boop's product, not a tenant's data — and the seed runs
neither on staging nor in production, so the form could never be opened on the
hosted environment (D-23). The seed keeps what is actually tenant data: the two
fictional clients' submissions and answers.

`onboarding_answers` gains a trigger that no policy could replace: the question
must belong to the template the submission answers, and the value must have the
shape of the question's type. Authorization derives from the SUBMISSION, which
says nothing about the QUESTION — so holding your own submission was enough to
store an answer pointing at another template's question. Trigger and not policy
because it is a data invariant: it holds for `service_role` too.

`onboarding_submissions` loses INSERT and UPDATE for `authenticated`. The
phase-4 UPDATE policy was designed to allow `draft -> submitted` directly, and
that premise was wrong: sending is status + stage + activity log, atomically. A
`client_user` could move the row through PostgREST with no journey and no audit
trail, and nothing in the system would know. The lifecycle is now
`start_onboarding()`, `submit_onboarding()` and `reopen_onboarding()` — and
nothing else, not even for Boop.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018oK4rvj1mYjUcJY54csxCy
